// Wholesale Hub call scripts (owner-only screen): ONE static lookup, not stored per lead and never sent through the lead upsert (no schema or database change).
// Keys match the pick order in hub-wholesale.js (deceased_estate > foreclosure_tax > llc > listing_agent > oh_homes > nm_land > nm_homes).
// Each text is the verbatim script from Dex's scripts/01-07_*.md (title line dropped; the panel shows the label). Items in [brackets] are filled in by Christian on the call; no owner data lives here.
// Source files: /workspace/dex/scripts/01-07_*.md (file name kept per key). To change a script, replace that key's text.
window.WS_SCRIPTS = Object.freeze({
  oh_homes: { label: "Cleveland, OH homeowner", title: "Script 1: Cleveland, OH homeowner (distressed property)", file: "01_cleveland_homeowner.md", text: "*Caller: Christian Moseley (live, himself). Plain, direct, friendly. Items in [brackets] come from the lead row. Lines in quotes are said out loud.*\n\n## 0. Before you dial (every call)\n- **Live human only.** You dial and talk yourself. No autodialer, no prerecorded or \"ringless\" voicemail, no AI voice.\n- **Scrub the number against the National Do Not Call registry first** (donotcall.gov), and against your own do-not-call list. If it hits either one, do not call.\n- **Time check, 8 AM to 9 PM in the owner's local time.** **Cleveland is Eastern time.** From Denver that is **6:00 AM to 7:00 PM Mountain.** If the owner is out of state (many are), use *their* local time if it is earlier or later. Look at a clock right before you dial. If you are unsure which zone the person is in (out-of-state owner, cell number), stay inside 9 AM to 8 PM in every zone it could be.\n- **Recording:** do not record unless the other person agrees. If you do record, say so at the start (see the line below). Ohio and New Mexico are one-party-consent states, but saying so is safest, and the other person may be in a different state.\n  - Say: \"I'd like to record this call just for my own notes. Is that okay?\" If no, don't record, and take notes by hand.\n- Have the lead row open (address/APN, owner, flags). Write the time you called and the outcome as you go.\n- Know the lead flags (you will not recite them): condemned / board-up / violations / tax delinquent / out of state. They are for your background only.\n\n**Rules for the whole call**\n- You are the **buyer**, always as **\"Christian Moseley and/or assigns.\"** Say it out loud and put it in writing (see the spoken disclosure below).\n- Never say you are an agent or broker. Never offer to list the property or \"find a buyer\" for a fee. You buy directly.\n- Nothing misleading: no made-up deadlines, no \"I have a buyer waiting,\" no \"my partners,\" no hiding that you may assign.\n- If they ask where you're based or whether you're local, answer truthfully and plainly.\n- No pressure. A calm \"no\" is a good outcome. Do not argue.\n\n## 1. Opener\n> \"Hi, is this [First name]? ... This is Christian Moseley. I'm an investor buying in Cleveland.\"\n\n(Pause. Let them answer.)\n\n## 2. Permission question\n> \"I know I'm calling out of the blue. Do you have a minute?\"\n\n- **Yes:** go to the reason.\n- **Bad time:** \"No problem at all. When's a better time, later today or another day?\" Write it down and end warmly.\n- **\"Who is this / what is this about?\":** \"Christian Moseley. I buy houses in Cleveland. It's about the house on [street name only]. Is that yours?\"\n- **Wrong person / not the owner:** \"Sorry about that. Do you know the best way to reach the owner?\" Take whatever they give. Don't push.\n\n## 3. Reason for the call (soft; public records; do not recite violations)\n> \"I look through the county's public property records for houses in Cleveland that might be a hassle for the owner, and yours at [street name] came up. I'm not calling about anything specific. I just wanted to ask if you'd ever thought about selling it.\"\n\nIf they live out of state, add: \"I see you're not living in Cleveland, and I know a house is harder to look after from far away.\"\n\n**Only if they bring up the city, the taxes or the condition:** let them tell it. If they ask what you saw, answer straight and short: \"Just what's in the public records: there's some city paperwork on it and a tax balance. I only know what's on paper, and you know the house far better than I do.\" Do not list counts, dates or dollar amounts. Never say \"your house is condemned\" or \"I saw your violations.\"\n\n## 4. Discovery questions (ask one, then listen. Don't interrupt.)\n1. **Condition:** \"What's the house like these days? Is anyone living in it, or is it empty?\" \"Is it boarded up, or has it had damage (roof, water, fire)?\" \"Have you been inside lately?\"\n2. **Timeline:** \"If you did sell, how soon would you want to be done? Is there a date you're up against?\"\n3. **Motivation:** \"What's made you think about selling, or what would be the best thing selling does for you?\" (Then be quiet.)\n4. **Mortgage / liens:** \"Is there a mortgage on it? Any liens or back taxes you know of?\" \"Is there anything pending with the city or the county, like a hearing, that I should know about?\" (Let them say it. Don't give legal advice.)\n5. **Price expectation:** \"Have you had a number in mind, or had it looked at by anyone?\" \"What would you need to see to feel good about it?\"\n6. **Decision makers:** \"Is it just you on the deed, or is anyone else involved, like a relative or a co-owner?\" \"Who would I need to talk to besides you?\" \"Is the owner still living? Is it in an estate?\" (If an estate: use Script 6.)\n7. **Out of state / access:** \"Where are you now? Who looks after the house for you?\" \"Is there a way to see it, or has someone been there?\"\n\nNote answers. Don't argue with any of them.\n\n## 5. Setting up the offer (no number on this call unless they push)\n> \"Here's how it works. I buy as-is, for cash, so you don't need to clean it out, fix it or make repairs. I'd like to look at the title and the numbers on the house, then send you a **written offer** within [2] business days. If you like it, we use a local title company and you can close in as little as [14 to 30] days, or on your schedule. There's no obligation and no fee to you. Can I send it by [email / mail]? What's the best address?\"\n\nConfirm: owner(s) who must sign, best email and mailing address, best time to call, any date they are up against.\n\n**If they push for a number** (say it once, as a range, with conditions):\n> \"I can't be exact yet. Properties like this usually land somewhere between $[LOW] and $[HIGH], depending on the title, any liens or back taxes, and what I find on the condition. That's not an offer. A written one will follow.\"\n(LOW and HIGH come from your own numbers. Never go outside them on the phone. Don't quote the ARV or what you'd sell it for.)\n\n**Ohio paperwork line** (say it here): \"Before any contract, Ohio law has me give you a separate disclosure form that you and I both sign and date. I'll send it with the paperwork and it explains how I work. Nothing is binding until you've signed it.\"\n\n## 6. Top 6 objections\n\n**1. \"How did you get my number / my name?\"**\n> \"Fair question. The owner's name is in the county's public property records, and I found the phone number through a public lookup. If you'd like, I'll take you off my list right now.\"\n\n**2. \"I'm not interested / it's not for sale.\"**\n> \"That's okay, and I appreciate you telling me straight. If anything changes, I buy as-is, for cash, so you don't have to fix anything. Can I check back in a few months, or would you rather I didn't?\"\n\n**3. \"What's it worth / what will you pay? / That's too low.\"**\n> \"I can't give you a good number without checking the title and running the numbers, and I don't want to throw out a guess that's wrong. Let me do that homework and send you a written offer. If it doesn't work, you've lost nothing. What would you want to see to make it worth it?\"\n\n**4. \"You're a wholesaler / you'll just flip it.\"**\n> \"I'm an investor and a buyer. I do buy as 'Christian Moseley and/or assigns,' so I may assign the contract to another buyer before closing, and I'll put that in writing before you sign anything. I'm not an agent and I'm not working for you. Ohio also has me give you a separate disclosure form, and you sign and date it before any contract. If someone else buys it, your price and terms don't change.\" (Don't deny it. Don't dodge.)\n\n**5. \"There's a mortgage / back taxes / the city has things on it. Can you still buy it?\"**\n> \"Maybe, and I can't promise. Title has to clear it. Liens and back taxes are normally paid out of the sale price at closing through a title company, and I'd look at what the numbers allow. I can't tell you what the city will or won't do, and I'm not a lawyer. A local attorney can speak to that. What I can do is look at it and send you a written offer.\"\n(Never promise to stop a demolition, fine or tax sale.)\n\n**6. \"I need to think about it / talk to my family / my lawyer.\"**\n> \"Of course, please do. There's no rush from my side. I'll send what I have in writing so they can see it too. Is it okay if I call you back on [day]?\"\n(Also covers \"Can't you just list it for me?\" -> \"I'm not an agent and I don't list houses. I buy directly. If you want it listed, a licensed agent is the right person.\")\n\n## 7. Spoken disclosure (say it before you end any call that is going toward an offer)\n> \"One thing I tell every seller up front: I buy as 'Christian Moseley and/or assigns.' That means I may assign the contract to another buyer before closing. I'll put that in writing in the paperwork I send, before you sign anything. I'm a buyer. I'm not an agent, and I'm not working for you.\"\n> **Ohio add-on:** \"Ohio law also requires me to give you a separate disclosure form, and both of us sign and date it before any contract. I'll send it with the paperwork. It explains how I work, and nothing is binding until that is signed. You're welcome to have a lawyer or someone you trust look at everything first.\"\n(ORC 5301.95, in effect March 2, 2026, for 1-4 unit residential property. Separate document, bold 12-point type, signed and dated by the owner and by you before any binding contract. If it is skipped, the owner can cancel before closing and it is an unfair or deceptive practice.)\n\n## 8. Voicemail (leave it live, you speaking; no prerecorded or ringless drops; 20 to 25 seconds; no price, no pressure)\n> \"Hi, this is Christian Moseley, an investor buying in Cleveland. I'm calling for [First name] about the house on [street name]. I'm not calling about anything urgent. If you ever thought about selling it, I buy as-is for cash. You can reach me at [phone number]. Again, Christian Moseley, [phone number]. Thanks, and have a good day.\"\n\n(Don't mention violations, taxes or foreclosure on a voicemail. Others may hear it.)\n\n## 9. Text-free follow-up note (mail or email only; do not text)\nSend only if they did not ask to stop, ideally same day or next. It also puts the assignment disclosure in writing.\n**Subject / top line:** Following up on the house at [address]\n\n> Hi [First name],\n>\n> This is Christian Moseley, the investor buying in Cleveland. [We spoke on [date]. / I tried to reach you on [date].] Thank you for your time.\n>\n> I buy houses as-is for cash and can close on your timeline. If you'd like a written offer, reply here or call me at [phone] and I'll run the numbers and send one.\n>\n> Two things I want you to know in writing:\n> 1. I buy as **\"Christian Moseley and/or assigns.\"** That means I may assign the contract to another buyer before closing. I'm a buyer. I'm not an agent and I don't represent you.\n> 2. Before any contract, Ohio law (ORC 5301.95) requires me to give you a separate disclosure form that both of us sign and date. I'll send it with any offer. Nothing is binding until it's signed. You're welcome to have an attorney review everything.\n>\n> No pressure either way. If you'd rather not hear from me, just tell me and I'll take you off my list.\n>\n> Christian Moseley\n[phone] | [email]\n[mailing address]\n\n## Do-not-call handling (use the second they ask, in any words)\n> \"Absolutely. I'm taking you off my list right now and you won't hear from me again. Sorry for the interruption. Take care.\"\n\nThen: hang up politely. Mark the number **DNC** in your own list and in the hub right away (same day, before the next dial). Don't ask why, don't make one last pitch, don't call again, and don't send a follow-up note. This also applies to \"stop calling,\" \"take me off,\" \"I'm on the do-not-call list,\" and \"don't contact me.\"\n\n## After the call\n- Log: date and time, outcome, motivation, timeline, price they mentioned, who decides, and any DNC. Put it in the hub notes **without the owner's name or address.**\n- Promised something? Do it the same day (written offer, paperwork, call-back date).\n- If you did not reach them, wait a couple of days before the next try. Cap it at a few tries, spaced out, then stop.\n" },
  nm_homes: { label: "New Mexico homeowner", title: "Script 2: New Mexico homeowner", file: "02_nm_homeowner.md", text: "*Caller: Christian Moseley (live, himself). Plain, direct, friendly. Items in [brackets] come from the lead row. Lines in quotes are said out loud.*\n\n## 0. Before you dial (every call)\n- **Live human only.** You dial and talk yourself. No autodialer, no prerecorded or \"ringless\" voicemail, no AI voice.\n- **Scrub the number against the National Do Not Call registry first** (donotcall.gov), and against your own do-not-call list. If it hits either one, do not call.\n- **Time check, 8 AM to 9 PM in the owner's local time.** **New Mexico is Mountain time** (same as you in Denver): **8:00 AM to 9:00 PM Mountain.** If the owner's number or mailing address is in another state, use their local time too. Look at a clock right before you dial. If you are unsure which zone the person is in (out-of-state owner, cell number), stay inside 9 AM to 8 PM in every zone it could be.\n- **Recording:** do not record unless the other person agrees. If you do record, say so at the start (see the line below). Ohio and New Mexico are one-party-consent states, but saying so is safest, and the other person may be in a different state.\n  - Say: \"I'd like to record this call just for my own notes. Is that okay?\" If no, don't record, and take notes by hand.\n- Have the lead row open (address/APN, owner, flags). Write the time you called and the outcome as you go.\n\n**Rules for the whole call**\n- You are the **buyer**, always as **\"Christian Moseley and/or assigns.\"** Say it out loud and put it in writing (see the spoken disclosure below).\n- Never say you are an agent or broker. Never offer to list the property or \"find a buyer\" for a fee. You buy directly.\n- Nothing misleading: no made-up deadlines, no \"I have a buyer waiting,\" no \"my partners,\" no hiding that you may assign.\n- If they ask where you're based or whether you're local, answer truthfully and plainly.\n- No pressure. A calm \"no\" is a good outcome. Do not argue.\n\n## 1. Opener\n> \"Hi, is this [First name]? This is Christian Moseley. I'm a local investor who buys homes here in New Mexico.\"\n\n## 2. Permission question\n> \"I know I'm calling out of the blue. Do you have a quick minute?\"\n\n- **Bad time:** \"No problem. When's better?\" Write it down and end warmly.\n- **\"Who is this?\":** \"Christian Moseley. I buy houses in New Mexico. It's about [street name]. Is that your place?\"\n- **Wrong person:** \"Sorry to bother you. Do you know the best way to reach the owner?\" Don't push.\n\n## 3. Reason for the call (soft, public records)\n> \"I look at public property records around [city/county] for homes where the owner might want to sell, and yours at [street name] came up. I'm not calling about anything in particular. I wanted to ask if you'd ever thought about selling it.\"\n\n(Use the lead's reason in plain words only if it's natural: \"the records show some past permit work on it,\" \"it looks like you may not live there full-time.\" Never read a list. If a tax balance shows and they bring it up, let them talk. If they ask, say \"I only know what's in the public records.\")\n\n## 4. Discovery questions\n1. **Condition:** \"What shape is the house in? Does it need work, like a roof, plumbing, or finishing something that was started?\" \"Is anyone living there, or is it a rental or empty?\"\n2. **Timeline:** \"If you sold, when would you want to be done? Is there a date that matters?\"\n3. **Motivation:** \"What's making you think about selling, or what would selling do for you?\"\n4. **Mortgage / liens:** \"Is there a mortgage on it? Any liens or unpaid property taxes you know about?\"\n5. **Price expectation:** \"Do you have a number in mind? Has anyone looked at it or priced it for you?\"\n6. **Decision makers:** \"Is it just you on the deed? Anyone else, like a spouse, a sibling or a co-owner, who'd weigh in?\" (If the owner has died: Script 6.)\n7. **Other:** \"Have you talked to an agent about it? What did you decide?\" \"Would you rather sell as-is, with no repairs and no showings?\"\n\n## 5. Setting up the offer (no number on this call unless they push)\n> \"Here's how it works. I buy as-is, for cash. No repairs, no cleaning out, no showings, and I don't charge you anything. I'll run my numbers and send you a **written offer** within [2] business days. If you like it, we use a local title company, and we can close in [14 to 30] days or on your schedule. There's no obligation. What's the best email or mailing address for it?\"\n\nConfirm: everyone who must sign, best email and mailing address, best time to call, any date they're up against.\n\n**If they push for a number** (once, as a range, with conditions):\n> \"I can't be exact yet. Homes like this usually land somewhere between $[LOW] and $[HIGH], depending on the title, any liens, and the condition. That's not an offer; a written one will follow.\"\n(Ranges come from your own numbers.)\n\n**NM:** no finder's fees. You do not take or promise any fee from the seller, and you do not offer to find them a buyer.\n\n## 6. Top 6 objections\n\n**1. \"How did you get my number?\"**\n> \"From public records and a public lookup. If you'd like, I'll take you off my list right now.\"\n\n**2. \"I'm not interested / not for sale.\"**\n> \"No problem, and thanks for being straight with me. If anything changes, I buy as-is for cash. Is it okay if I check back in a few months, or would you rather I didn't?\"\n\n**3. \"Make me an offer now / that's too low.\"**\n> \"I want to give you a real number, not a guess. Let me look at the property records and run my numbers, and I'll send you a written offer. If it doesn't work for you, you're out nothing. What number would make you comfortable?\"\n\n**4. \"I'd get more listing it with an agent.\"**\n> \"That could be true, and an agent is a good choice for some people. A listing means repairs, showings and waiting. I'm a buyer, not an agent, and I don't list houses. If an as-is cash sale with a quick close is useful to you, I'm happy to give you an offer to compare.\"\n\n**5. \"You're a wholesaler.\"**\n> \"I'm an investor and the buyer. I buy as 'Christian Moseley and/or assigns,' so I may assign the contract to another buyer before closing. I'll tell you that in writing before you sign anything. I charge you no fee or commission, and your price and terms stay the same either way.\"\n\n**6. \"I need to talk to my spouse / family / a lawyer.\"**\n> \"Of course. Take your time. I'll send the details in writing so they can read it too. Is it okay if I call back on [day]?\"\n\n## 7. Spoken disclosure (say it before you end any call that is going toward an offer)\n> \"One thing I tell every seller up front: I buy as 'Christian Moseley and/or assigns.' That means I may assign the contract to another buyer before closing. I'll put that in writing in the paperwork I send, before you sign anything. I'm a buyer. I'm not an agent, and I'm not working for you.\"\n> **New Mexico add-on:** \"I don't charge you any fee or commission. I'm not an agent and I'm not finding a buyer for you for a fee. I'm buying it myself, and I may assign my contract.\"\n(No finder's fees in NM: do not take or promise any fee from the seller or for \"finding\" anyone.)\n\n## 8. Voicemail (leave it live, you speaking; no prerecorded or ringless drops; 20 to 25 seconds; no price, no pressure)\n> \"Hi, this is Christian Moseley, a local investor who buys homes in New Mexico. I'm calling for [First name] about the property on [street name]. Nothing urgent. If you ever thought about selling, I buy as-is for cash. You can reach me at [phone number]. That's Christian Moseley, [phone number]. Thanks!\"\n\n## 9. Text-free follow-up note (mail or email only; do not text)\nSend only if they did not ask to stop, ideally same day or next. It also puts the assignment disclosure in writing.\n**Subject / top line:** Following up about [street name]\n\n> Hi [First name],\n>\n> This is Christian Moseley, the local investor who buys homes in New Mexico. [We spoke on [date]. / I tried to reach you on [date].] Thanks for your time.\n>\n> I buy as-is for cash with a quick close, and I charge you no fee or commission. If you'd like a written offer, reply here or call me at [phone], and I'll run the numbers and send one.\n>\n> In writing: I buy as **\"Christian Moseley and/or assigns.\"** That means I may assign the contract to another buyer before closing. I'm a buyer. I'm not an agent and I don't represent you. You're welcome to have an attorney review anything I send.\n>\n> If you'd rather not hear from me, just say so and I'll take you off my list.\n>\n> Christian Moseley\n[phone] | [email]\n[mailing address]\n\n## Do-not-call handling (use the second they ask, in any words)\n> \"Absolutely. I'm taking you off my list right now and you won't hear from me again. Sorry for the interruption. Take care.\"\n\nThen: hang up politely. Mark the number **DNC** in your own list and in the hub right away (same day, before the next dial). Don't ask why, don't make one last pitch, don't call again, and don't send a follow-up note. This also applies to \"stop calling,\" \"take me off,\" \"I'm on the do-not-call list,\" and \"don't contact me.\"\n\n## After the call\n- Log: date and time, outcome, motivation, timeline, price they mentioned, who decides, and any DNC. Put it in the hub notes **without the owner's name or address.**\n- Promised something? Do it the same day (written offer, paperwork, call-back date).\n- If you did not reach them, wait a couple of days before the next try. Cap it at a few tries, spaced out, then stop.\n" },
  nm_land: { label: "New Mexico vacant land", title: "Script 3: New Mexico vacant land owner (Rio Rancho Estates lots)", file: "03_nm_vacant_land.md", text: "*Caller: Christian Moseley (live, himself). Plain, direct, friendly. Items in [brackets] come from the lead row. Lines in quotes are said out loud.*\n\n## 0. Before you dial (every call)\n- **Live human only.** You dial and talk yourself. No autodialer, no prerecorded or \"ringless\" voicemail, no AI voice.\n- **Scrub the number against the National Do Not Call registry first** (donotcall.gov), and against your own do-not-call list. If it hits either one, do not call.\n- **Time check, 8 AM to 9 PM in the owner's local time.** **New Mexico is Mountain time**: **8:00 AM to 9:00 PM Mountain.** Many lot owners live out of state, so check the owner's local time from the lead's mailing state (`mail_state`) and the area code, and stay inside 8 AM to 9 PM there too. Look at a clock right before you dial. If you are unsure which zone the person is in (out-of-state owner, cell number), stay inside 9 AM to 8 PM in every zone it could be.\n- **Recording:** do not record unless the other person agrees. If you do record, say so at the start (see the line below). Ohio and New Mexico are one-party-consent states, but saying so is safest, and the other person may be in a different state.\n  - Say: \"I'd like to record this call just for my own notes. Is that okay?\" If no, don't record, and take notes by hand.\n- Have the lead row open (address/APN, owner, flags). Write the time you called and the outcome as you go.\n- Open the row in deals.csv / the hub: Unit, Block, Lot, APN, acres, tax status, deed flags. Opening and walk-away numbers are **provisional** until a builder buy box is confirmed. Do not state a builder price.\n\n**Rules for the whole call**\n- You are the **buyer**, always as **\"Christian Moseley and/or assigns.\"** Say it out loud and put it in writing (see the spoken disclosure below).\n- Never say you are an agent or broker. Never offer to list the property or \"find a buyer\" for a fee. You buy directly.\n- Nothing misleading: no made-up deadlines, no \"I have a buyer waiting,\" no \"my partners,\" no hiding that you may assign.\n- If they ask where you're based or whether you're local, answer truthfully and plainly.\n- No pressure. A calm \"no\" is a good outcome. Do not argue.\n\n## 1. Opener\n> \"Hi, is this [First name]? This is Christian Moseley. I'm a local investor, and I'm buying lots in Rio Rancho Estates.\"\n\n## 2. Permission question\n> \"I'm calling about a vacant lot you own out there. Do you have a minute?\"\n\n- **Bad time:** \"No problem. When's better?\" Note it.\n- **\"Which lot?\":** \"Rio Rancho Estates, Unit [UNIT], Block [BLK], Lot [LOT]. The county has it in your name.\"\n- **Not the owner:** \"Sorry about that. Do you know the best way to reach the owner?\"\n\n## 3. Reason for the call (soft, public records)\n> \"I'm buying lots in Rio Rancho Estates, and the Sandoval County records show you as the owner of Unit [UNIT], Block [BLK], Lot [LOT]. I wanted to ask if you'd ever thought about selling it.\"\n\n(If the lead shows back taxes, don't open with it. If they bring it up, or ask what you saw: \"The records show a tax balance. I only know what's on paper.\" Say it kindly. No lecture.)\n\n## 4. Discovery questions\n1. **Still the owner?** \"Is the lot still in your name, or in a trust or an estate?\" (If deceased or an estate: \"I'm sorry to hear it. Who handles it now? Could you point me to that person?\" See Script 6.)\n2. **Motivation:** \"Have you ever thought about selling it?\" \"What made you buy it originally?\" \"What would selling it do for you?\"\n3. **Timeline:** \"If you sold it, how soon would you want to be done?\"\n4. **Mortgage / liens / taxes:** \"Is there a mortgage or any lien on it?\" (Free and clear: they may like quick cash or terms.) \"Are you caught up on the property taxes?\" (Only kindly, only if the lead shows a balance.)\n5. **Condition / access:** \"Have you been out to see it? Do you know about road access, power or water at the lot?\" (Note the answers. Don't promise anything about utilities or buildability.)\n6. **Price expectation:** \"Do you have a number in mind? What did you pay, or what has anyone told you it's worth?\"\n7. **Decision makers:** \"Is it just you on the deed? Anyone else I'd need to talk to?\"\n\n## 5. Setting up the offer (no number on this call unless they push)\n> \"Here's how it works. I pay cash, as-is, and close fast through a local title company. I cover [the normal closing costs; confirm in writing] and you don't need to travel. I'll look at the parcel and the title and send you a **written offer** within [2] business days. No obligation. What's the best email or mailing address?\"\n\n**If they push for a number** (once, as a range):\n> \"I can't be exact yet. Lots like this usually land between $[LOW] and $[HIGH], depending on the taxes, access and the title. That's not an offer; a written one will follow.\"\n(Range from the deals.csv opening and walk-away, which are **provisional**. Move in small steps. Ask first: \"What number would make you comfortable?\" Never state a builder price, and don't say you have a builder or end buyer lined up.)\n\n**NM:** no finder's fees. You do not take or promise any fee from the seller, and you do not offer to find them a buyer.\n\n## 6. Top 6 objections\n\n**1. \"How did you get my number / my info?\"**\n> \"From public records and a public lookup. The county lists the owner of each lot. If you'd like, I'll take you off my list right now.\"\n\n**2. \"Make it higher / that's too low.\"**\n> \"I understand. What price were you hoping for? Let me see what I can do. These lots have no city water or sewer, limited road or power in many areas, and some carry back taxes. I'm buying as-is, with no cleanup, no fees and no agent commission for you.\" (Say it only as it applies to this lot.)\n\n**3. \"I get offers all the time / I'm waiting for the price to go up.\"**\n> \"Fair enough. Rio Rancho is growing, and no one knows what the price will do. I'm cash, there's no financing contingency, and you pay nothing at closing. If you'd rather hold, that's fine. Can I check back in a few months?\"\n\n**4. \"You sound like a wholesaler.\"**\n> \"I buy lots, and I buy as 'Christian Moseley and/or assigns,' so I may assign the contract to another buyer. I'm telling you up front, and I'll put it in writing. Your price and terms won't change either way. I'm a buyer, not an agent, and I don't charge you any fee.\"\n\n**5. \"I still owe taxes / there's a lien / the title is messy.\"**\n> \"That's common with these lots. Back taxes and liens are normally paid from the sale price at closing through a title company, and I'd look at it before I make an offer. I can't promise how it comes out until the title company checks it.\"\n\n**6. \"I need to think about it / ask my spouse / my kids.\"**\n> \"Of course. There's no rush. I'll send the details in writing so everyone can read them. Can I check back on [day]?\"\n\n## 7. Spoken disclosure (say it before you end any call that is going toward an offer)\n> \"One thing I tell every seller up front: I buy as 'Christian Moseley and/or assigns.' That means I may assign the contract to another buyer before closing. I'll put that in writing in the paperwork I send, before you sign anything. I'm a buyer. I'm not an agent, and I'm not working for you.\"\n> **New Mexico add-on:** \"I don't charge you any fee or commission. I'm not an agent and I'm not finding a buyer for you for a fee. I'm buying it myself, and I may assign my contract.\"\n(No finder's fees in NM: do not take or promise any fee from the seller or for \"finding\" anyone.)\n\n## 8. Voicemail (leave it live, you speaking; no prerecorded or ringless drops; 20 to 25 seconds; no price, no pressure)\n> \"Hi, this is Christian Moseley. I'm a local investor buying lots in Rio Rancho Estates. I'm calling for [First name] about a vacant lot in your name out there. If you ever thought about selling it, I pay cash and close fast. You can reach me at [phone number]. That's Christian Moseley, [phone number]. Thanks!\"\n\n## 9. Text-free follow-up note (mail or email only; do not text)\nSend only if they did not ask to stop, ideally same day or next. It also puts the assignment disclosure in writing.\n**Subject / top line:** Your lot in Rio Rancho Estates (Unit [UNIT], Block [BLK], Lot [LOT])\n\n> Hi [First name],\n>\n> This is Christian Moseley, the investor buying lots in Rio Rancho Estates. [We spoke on [date]. / I tried to reach you on [date].] Thank you for your time.\n>\n> I pay cash, buy as-is, and close through a local title company. You don't need to travel. If you'd like a written offer, reply here or call me at [phone].\n>\n> In writing: I buy as **\"Christian Moseley and/or assigns.\"** That means I may assign the contract to another buyer before closing. Your price and terms would not change. I'm a buyer. I'm not an agent, and I charge you no fee.\n>\n> If you'd rather not hear from me, just tell me and I'll take you off my list.\n>\n> Christian Moseley\n[phone] | [email]\n[mailing address]\n\n## Do-not-call handling (use the second they ask, in any words)\n> \"Absolutely. I'm taking you off my list right now and you won't hear from me again. Sorry for the interruption. Take care.\"\n\nThen: hang up politely. Mark the number **DNC** in your own list and in the hub right away (same day, before the next dial). Don't ask why, don't make one last pitch, don't call again, and don't send a follow-up note. This also applies to \"stop calling,\" \"take me off,\" \"I'm on the do-not-call list,\" and \"don't contact me.\"\n\n## After the call\n- Log: date and time, outcome, motivation, timeline, price they mentioned, who decides, and any DNC. Put it in the hub notes **without the owner's name or address.**\n- Promised something? Do it the same day (written offer, paperwork, call-back date).\n- If you did not reach them, wait a couple of days before the next try. Cap it at a few tries, spaced out, then stop.\n" },
  listing_agent: { label: "Listing agent", title: "Script 4: Listing agent on a listed property (investor buyer, cash offer)", file: "04_listing_agent.md", text: "*Caller: Christian Moseley (live, himself). Plain, direct, friendly. Items in [brackets] come from the lead row. Lines in quotes are said out loud.*\n\n## 0. Before you dial (every call)\n- **Live human only.** You dial and talk yourself. No autodialer, no prerecorded or \"ringless\" voicemail, no AI voice.\n- **Scrub the number against the National Do Not Call registry first** (donotcall.gov), and against your own do-not-call list. If it hits either one, do not call.\n- **Time check, 8 AM to 9 PM in the owner's local time.** Use the **agent's local time** (and the property's, if different). **Cleveland is Eastern (6 AM to 7 PM Mountain for you); New Mexico is Mountain.** Agents' business lines may be outside the registry's reach, but scrub anyway, honor any \"don't call,\" and keep it to business hours. Look at a clock right before you dial. If you are unsure which zone the person is in (out-of-state owner, cell number), stay inside 9 AM to 8 PM in every zone it could be.\n- **Recording:** do not record unless the other person agrees. If you do record, say so at the start (see the line below). Ohio and New Mexico are one-party-consent states, but saying so is safest, and the other person may be in a different state.\n  - Say: \"I'd like to record this call just for my own notes. Is that okay?\" If no, don't record, and take notes by hand.\n- Have the lead row open (address/APN, owner, flags). Write the time you called and the outcome as you go.\n- Have the listing open: address, list price, days on market, remarks. Know your own limit and your closing window before you speak. Only say \"cash\" and \"proof of funds\" if you can back them up in writing.\n- You are an **unrepresented principal buyer**, not an agent. Offers go through the listing agent. **Do not contact the seller directly.**\n\n**Rules for the whole call**\n- You are the **buyer**, always as **\"Christian Moseley and/or assigns.\"** Say it out loud and put it in writing (see the spoken disclosure below).\n- Never say you are an agent or broker. Never offer to list the property or \"find a buyer\" for a fee. You buy directly.\n- Nothing misleading: no made-up deadlines, no \"I have a buyer waiting,\" no \"my partners,\" no hiding that you may assign.\n- If they ask where you're based or whether you're local, answer truthfully and plainly.\n- No pressure. A calm \"no\" is a good outcome. Do not argue.\n\n## 1. Opener\n> \"Hi, is this [Agent name]? This is Christian Moseley. I'm an investor buyer. I buy for cash and close quickly.\"\n\n## 2. Permission question\n> \"I'm calling about your listing at [address]. Do you have a minute?\"\n\n- **Busy:** \"No problem. When's a good time?\"\n- **Agent says call someone else:** \"Thanks. Who should I talk to?\"\n\n## 3. Reason for the call\n> \"I'm interested in the property at [address] and I'd like to make a cash offer. Before I write it up, I'd like to ask you a few questions so the offer fits the seller's situation.\"\n\n(No tricks. You are calling because the listing is public, and you want to buy it.)\n\n## 4. Discovery questions\n1. **Status:** \"Is it still available? Any offers, or anything under contract or pending?\" \"How long has it been on the market?\"\n2. **Seller motivation and timeline:** \"What's the seller's situation? Is there a date they need to close by?\"\n3. **Condition and access:** \"How is it showing? Anything the listing doesn't tell me, like repairs, damage, tenants, or a lockbox?\" \"Can I see it, and when?\"\n4. **Occupancy:** \"Is it vacant, owner-occupied or rented?\"\n5. **Title, liens and authority:** \"Any liens, back taxes, HOA issues, probate or other owners who need to sign? Is the seller the sole owner?\"\n6. **Price:** \"Is the seller firm on the list price, or is there room for a quick cash close? Anything the seller has said about the number?\"\n7. **Offer process:** \"How do you want offers submitted, and is there a deadline or a best-and-final date?\" \"What do you need from me: proof of funds, earnest money, a pre-approval letter?\"\n8. **Compensation:** \"I'm not a licensee and not represented. How does the seller's listing agreement handle the buyer's side? I want my offer to reflect that correctly.\"\n9. **Decision makers:** \"Who signs for the seller, and will they see every offer?\"\n\n## 5. Setting up the offer (no number on the first call unless they push)\n> \"Thanks, that's helpful. I'll run my numbers and send you a **written offer** by [time/date]. It will be from 'Christian Moseley and/or assigns,' all cash, as-is, with a [14 to 21] day close, a [short] inspection period, and earnest money of $[amount] with a title company you choose. The assignment language will be in the contract, and I'd ask you to pass it on to the seller. What email should I use, and who at the title company?\"\n\n**If they push for a number** (once, as a range):\n> \"Right now I'd say between $[LOW] and $[HIGH], depending on what I see in the title and the condition. That's not an offer; the written one will follow.\"\n\n**Paperwork to send with the offer**\n- Purchase contract in the name **\"Christian Moseley and/or assigns,\"** with clear assignment language.\n- Proof of funds (only if real).\n- **Ohio property (1-4 unit residential):** the separate ORC 5301.95 disclosure, bold 12-point, for the **record owner** to sign and date before any binding contract; ask the agent to deliver it. The seller can cancel before closing if it was skipped.\n- **NM property:** no fee to the seller, and no finder's fee for anyone.\n\n## 6. Top 6 objections\n\n**1. \"Is this a lowball? Investors just send junk offers.\"**\n> \"I get why you'd think that. I'm not trying to waste your time. I want to know what the seller needs first, then write a clean offer: cash, as-is, a short close, and no financing contingency. If it's not close, I'd rather know now.\"\n\n**2. \"The seller wants full price / list price.\"**\n> \"Understood. Is there any room for a quick, clean cash close, no repairs and no financing risk? I'd rather hear that straight than guess. Let me see what the numbers allow, and I'll send something in writing.\"\n\n**3. \"Do you have proof of funds? Are you really cash?\"**\n> \"Yes. I can provide proof of funds with the offer.\" (Only say this if true, and send it. If funds come from a private lender, say so truthfully when asked.)\n\n**4. \"We already have offers / it's best and final.\"**\n> \"Got it. What's the deadline, and what matters most to the seller: price, speed, or certainty? I can put in a clean cash offer on that timeline.\"\n\n**5. \"Are you going to assign the contract? The seller needs to know.\"**\n> \"Yes, and you're right to ask. I buy as 'Christian Moseley and/or assigns,' so I may assign the contract to another buyer before closing. I'm telling you up front, and it will be in the offer in writing. Please make sure the seller sees it. In Ohio, I'll also send a separate signed-and-dated disclosure form before any binding contract, and I'd like it to go to the seller through you.\"\n\n**6. \"I only work with buyers who have an agent / have your agent call me.\"**\n> \"Understood. I'm buying directly and I'm not a licensee. I'm happy to submit a written offer through you and work with your title company. If the seller or you'd rather I use an agent, I'll consider it. I'm not asking you to do anything outside your listing agreement.\"\n(Also: never say you are an agent. Never ask them to \"find you a buyer\" or promise a fee to anyone for that. If they ask \"can you list it for me or refer a buyer,\" say: \"I'm not an agent and I don't list; I only buy.\")\n\n## 7. Spoken disclosure (say it before you end any call that is going toward an offer)\n> \"One thing I tell every seller up front: I buy as 'Christian Moseley and/or assigns.' That means I may assign the contract to another buyer before closing. I'll put that in writing in the paperwork I send, before you sign anything. I'm a buyer. I'm not an agent, and I'm not working for you.\"\n> **If the property is in Ohio:** \"Ohio law also requires me to give you a separate disclosure form, and both of us sign and date it before any contract. I'll send it with the paperwork and it explains how I work. Nothing is binding until that is signed.\" (ORC 5301.95, in effect March 2, 2026, 1-4 unit residential: separate document, bold 12-point type, signed and dated by the owner and by you before any binding contract.)\n> **If the property is in New Mexico:** \"I don't charge you any fee or commission, and I'm not finding a buyer for you for a fee.\" (No finder's fees in NM.)\n\n## 8. Voicemail (leave it live, you speaking; no prerecorded or ringless drops; 20 to 25 seconds; no price, no pressure)\n> \"Hi [Agent name], this is Christian Moseley, an investor buyer. I'm calling about your listing at [address]. I'm interested in making a cash offer with a quick close. You can reach me at [phone number]. That's Christian Moseley, [phone number]. Thanks.\"\n\n## 9. Text-free follow-up note (mail or email only; do not text)\nSend only if they did not ask to stop, ideally same day or next. It also puts the assignment disclosure in writing.\n**Subject:** Cash offer interest: [address]\n\n> Hi [Agent name],\n>\n> This is Christian Moseley. [Thanks for your time on [date]. / I tried to reach you on [date].] I'm interested in [address] and I'd like to submit a cash offer with a quick close.\n>\n> Please send me the offer deadline and anything the seller needs to see, and I'll get a written offer to you by [date].\n>\n> One thing in writing: I buy as **\"Christian Moseley and/or assigns,\"** so the contract may be assigned to another buyer before closing. Please share this with the seller. I'm an unrepresented principal buyer, not a licensee. If the property is in Ohio, I'll send the separate disclosure required by ORC 5301.95 with the offer, for the owner to sign and date before any binding contract.\n>\n> Christian Moseley\n[phone] | [email]\n[mailing address]\n\n## Do-not-call handling (use the second they ask, in any words)\n> \"Absolutely. I'm taking you off my list right now and you won't hear from me again. Sorry for the interruption. Take care.\"\n\nThen: hang up politely. Mark the number **DNC** in your own list and in the hub right away (same day, before the next dial). Don't ask why, don't make one last pitch, don't call again, and don't send a follow-up note. This also applies to \"stop calling,\" \"take me off,\" \"I'm on the do-not-call list,\" and \"don't contact me.\"\n\n## After the call\n- Log: date and time, outcome, motivation, timeline, price they mentioned, who decides, and any DNC. Put it in the hub notes **without the owner's name or address.**\n- Promised something? Do it the same day (written offer, paperwork, call-back date).\n- If you did not reach them, wait a couple of days before the next try. Cap it at a few tries, spaced out, then stop.\n" },
  llc: { label: "LLC or company owner", title: "Script 5: LLC or company owner", file: "05_llc_owner.md", text: "*Caller: Christian Moseley (live, himself). Plain, direct, friendly. Items in [brackets] come from the lead row. Lines in quotes are said out loud.*\n\n## 0. Before you dial (every call)\n- **Live human only.** You dial and talk yourself. No autodialer, no prerecorded or \"ringless\" voicemail, no AI voice.\n- **Scrub the number against the National Do Not Call registry first** (donotcall.gov), and against your own do-not-call list. If it hits either one, do not call.\n- **Time check, 8 AM to 9 PM in the owner's local time.** Use the **time zone of the person you're calling**. For an LLC, that may differ from the property (the number may be an office or a registered agent). **Eastern: 6 AM to 7 PM Mountain for you. Mountain (NM): 8 AM to 9 PM.** If unsure, stay inside 9 AM to 8 PM in every zone it could be. Look at a clock right before you dial. If you are unsure which zone the person is in (out-of-state owner, cell number), stay inside 9 AM to 8 PM in every zone it could be.\n- **Recording:** do not record unless the other person agrees. If you do record, say so at the start (see the line below). Ohio and New Mexico are one-party-consent states, but saying so is safest, and the other person may be in a different state.\n  - Say: \"I'd like to record this call just for my own notes. Is that okay?\" If no, don't record, and take notes by hand.\n- Have the lead row open (address/APN, owner, flags). Write the time you called and the outcome as you go.\n- Check public records first: the owner entity name, mailing address, and the Secretary of State listing (members/managers, registered agent). Many LLC numbers are personal cell phones, so scrub anyway.\n\n**Rules for the whole call**\n- You are the **buyer**, always as **\"Christian Moseley and/or assigns.\"** Say it out loud and put it in writing (see the spoken disclosure below).\n- Never say you are an agent or broker. Never offer to list the property or \"find a buyer\" for a fee. You buy directly.\n- Nothing misleading: no made-up deadlines, no \"I have a buyer waiting,\" no \"my partners,\" no hiding that you may assign.\n- If they ask where you're based or whether you're local, answer truthfully and plainly.\n- No pressure. A calm \"no\" is a good outcome. Do not argue.\n\n## 1. Opener\n> \"Hi, this is Christian Moseley. I'm a local investor. May I speak with whoever handles the properties for [LLC name]?\"\n\nIf they ask \"what is it about?\": \"I'm interested in a property the company owns on [street name]. I'd like to ask a few questions, if that's okay.\"\n\nIf you get the right person: \"Hi [Name], this is Christian Moseley. I'm a local investor.\" (Cleveland: \"an investor buying in Cleveland.\")\n\n## 2. Permission question\n> \"Do you have a minute?\"\n\n- **Gatekeeper won't connect you:** \"Understood. May I leave my name and number? Christian Moseley, [phone]. It's about the [street name] property.\" Don't pretend to be anyone or imply a prior relationship.\n- **Bad time:** \"No problem. When's better?\"\n\n## 3. Reason for the call (soft, public records)\n> \"The county records show [LLC name] owns [street name], and I buy properties like that. I wasn't sure if the company had thought about selling it, so I thought I'd ask.\"\n\n(Don't recite violations, taxes or tenant issues. If they bring them up, let them talk.)\n\n## 4. Discovery questions\n1. **Decision makers:** \"Are you the one who decides on this property? Who else is involved, like partners, members or an investor group?\" \"Is there an operating agreement that says who signs for the company?\"\n2. **Motivation:** \"Is the company still planning to hold it, or has that changed?\" \"Why did you buy it, and how's it going?\"\n3. **Condition:** \"How is the property doing? Does it need work? Any damage or code or city issues, as far as you know?\"\n4. **Occupancy:** \"Is it vacant, rented, or being worked on? If tenants, what do the leases say?\"\n5. **Mortgage / liens:** \"Is there a mortgage or other lender? Any liens or back taxes?\" (Lender consent or a payoff may be required.)\n6. **Timeline:** \"If the company sold, when would it want to be done?\"\n7. **Price expectation:** \"Do you have a number in mind? Has anyone looked at it or priced it?\"\n8. **Authority:** \"Who signs for the company? I'd want something in writing showing who has authority.\" (You'll ask for an authorization document or operating agreement excerpt, and a title company will typically ask too.)\n\n## 5. Setting up the offer (no number on the first call unless they push)\n> \"Here's how I'd handle it. I'll look at the title and the numbers, then send a **written offer** within [2 to 3] business days, from 'Christian Moseley and/or assigns.' It's cash and as-is, and we can close with a title company on a schedule that fits the company. I'll also need something showing who can sign for [LLC name]. What's the best email?\"\n\n**If they push for a number** (once, as a range):\n> \"Right now I'd say between $[LOW] and $[HIGH], depending on title, liens, leases and condition. That's not an offer; a written one will follow.\"\n\n**Paperwork:** the contract in the name \"Christian Moseley and/or assigns,\" with assignment language. **Ohio 1-4 unit residential:** send the separate ORC 5301.95 disclosure (bold 12-point, signed and dated before any binding contract) for the record owner's authorized signer. The statute does not exempt entity owners on its face, so send it. Ask an Ohio attorney if you need certainty. **NM:** no finder's fees.\n\n## 6. Top 6 objections\n\n**1. \"Who are you / how did you get this number?\"**\n> \"I'm Christian Moseley, a local investor. I found the owner name in public county records and the number through a public lookup. If you'd like, I'll take you off my list right now.\"\n\n**2. \"We're not selling / we're holding it.\"**\n> \"Understood, and thanks for being direct. If that changes, I buy as-is for cash. Is it okay if I check back in a few months?\"\n\n**3. \"What's your offer? What's your price?\"**\n> \"I don't want to give a number I haven't backed up. I'll look at the title and the numbers and send a written offer. Tell me what the company would need to see to make it worthwhile.\"\n\n**4. \"You're a wholesaler.\"**\n> \"I'm an investor and the buyer. I buy as 'Christian Moseley and/or assigns,' so I may assign the contract before closing. I'll say that in writing before anything is signed. I'm not an agent and I don't represent the company.\"\n\n**5. \"Talk to my partner / our attorney / send it in an email.\"**\n> \"Of course. Who's the best person, and what's the best email? I'll send a short written summary, and they can call me with questions.\"\n\n**6. \"There's a tenant / it's occupied / it's in the middle of a project.\"**\n> \"Thanks for telling me. I'm fine with that. I can buy it subject to the lease, or on a timeline that works. I'd want to see the leases and look at what the numbers allow before making any offer.\"\n\n## 7. Spoken disclosure (say it before you end any call that is going toward an offer)\n> \"One thing I tell every seller up front: I buy as 'Christian Moseley and/or assigns.' That means I may assign the contract to another buyer before closing. I'll put that in writing in the paperwork I send, before you sign anything. I'm a buyer. I'm not an agent, and I'm not working for you.\"\n> **If the property is in Ohio:** \"Ohio law also requires me to give you a separate disclosure form, and both of us sign and date it before any contract. I'll send it with the paperwork and it explains how I work. Nothing is binding until that is signed.\" (ORC 5301.95, in effect March 2, 2026, 1-4 unit residential: separate document, bold 12-point type, signed and dated by the owner and by you before any binding contract.)\n> **If the property is in New Mexico:** \"I don't charge you any fee or commission, and I'm not finding a buyer for you for a fee.\" (No finder's fees in NM.)\n\n## 8. Voicemail (leave it live, you speaking; no prerecorded or ringless drops; 20 to 25 seconds; no price, no pressure)\n> \"Hi, this is Christian Moseley, a local investor. I'm calling for whoever handles [LLC name]'s properties, about the property on [street name]. If the company ever thought about selling, I buy for cash and close fast. You can reach me at [phone number]. That's Christian Moseley, [phone number]. Thanks.\"\n\n## 9. Text-free follow-up note (mail or email only; do not text)\nSend only if they did not ask to stop, ideally same day or next. It also puts the assignment disclosure in writing.\n**Subject:** About [street name]\n\n> Hi [Name / \"to whoever handles [LLC name]'s properties\"],\n>\n> This is Christian Moseley, a local investor. [We spoke on [date]. / I tried to reach you on [date].] I'm interested in [street name].\n>\n> I buy as-is for cash and can close on the company's schedule. If it would help, reply here or call me at [phone], and I'll send a written offer after I've run the numbers.\n>\n> In writing: I buy as **\"Christian Moseley and/or assigns,\"** so I may assign the contract to another buyer before closing. I'm a buyer. I'm not an agent and I don't represent the company. If the property is a 1-4 unit residential property in Ohio, I'll send the separate disclosure required by ORC 5301.95 before any contract.\n>\n> Christian Moseley\n[phone] | [email]\n[mailing address]\n\n## Do-not-call handling (use the second they ask, in any words)\n> \"Absolutely. I'm taking you off my list right now and you won't hear from me again. Sorry for the interruption. Take care.\"\n\nThen: hang up politely. Mark the number **DNC** in your own list and in the hub right away (same day, before the next dial). Don't ask why, don't make one last pitch, don't call again, and don't send a follow-up note. This also applies to \"stop calling,\" \"take me off,\" \"I'm on the do-not-call list,\" and \"don't contact me.\"\n\n## After the call\n- Log: date and time, outcome, motivation, timeline, price they mentioned, who decides, and any DNC. Put it in the hub notes **without the owner's name or address.**\n- Promised something? Do it the same day (written offer, paperwork, call-back date).\n- If you did not reach them, wait a couple of days before the next try. Cap it at a few tries, spaced out, then stop.\n" },
  deceased_estate: { label: "Deceased owner (estate or heirs)", title: "Script 6: Deceased owner: estate or heirs", file: "06_deceased_estate_heirs.md", text: "*Caller: Christian Moseley (live, himself). Plain, direct, friendly. Items in [brackets] come from the lead row. Lines in quotes are said out loud.*\n\n## 0. Before you dial (every call)\n- **Live human only.** You dial and talk yourself. No autodialer, no prerecorded or \"ringless\" voicemail, no AI voice.\n- **Scrub the number against the National Do Not Call registry first** (donotcall.gov), and against your own do-not-call list. If it hits either one, do not call.\n- **Time check, 8 AM to 9 PM in the owner's local time.** Use the **local time of the person you're calling** (family members are often in other states). **Eastern: 6 AM to 7 PM Mountain for you. Mountain (NM): 8 AM to 9 PM.** For this call, go later than you'd go otherwise: skip early mornings and late evenings. Stay inside 9 AM to 8 PM. Look at a clock right before you dial. If you are unsure which zone the person is in (out-of-state owner, cell number), stay inside 9 AM to 8 PM in every zone it could be.\n- **Recording:** do not record unless the other person agrees. If you do record, say so at the start (see the line below). Ohio and New Mexico are one-party-consent states, but saying so is safest, and the other person may be in a different state.\n  - Say: \"I'd like to record this call just for my own notes. Is that okay?\" If no, don't record, and take notes by hand.\n- Have the lead row open (address/APN, owner, flags). Write the time you called and the outcome as you go.\n- Know what the records say (name on deed, any probate case, any tax balance) but **do not recite it.** You are calling someone who may be grieving. Slow down, soften your voice, and be ready to end the call kindly.\n- You are not a lawyer. Never say who can sign. Only a lawyer or the court can say.\n\n**Rules for the whole call**\n- You are the **buyer**, always as **\"Christian Moseley and/or assigns.\"** Say it out loud and put it in writing (see the spoken disclosure below).\n- Never say you are an agent or broker. Never offer to list the property or \"find a buyer\" for a fee. You buy directly.\n- Nothing misleading: no made-up deadlines, no \"I have a buyer waiting,\" no \"my partners,\" no hiding that you may assign.\n- If they ask where you're based or whether you're local, answer truthfully and plainly.\n- No pressure. A calm \"no\" is a good outcome. Do not argue.\n\n## 1. Opener\n> \"Hi, my name is Christian Moseley. I'm a local investor. I'm trying to reach whoever looks after the property at [street name]. It was in [Owner's name]'s name. Am I speaking with the right person?\"\n\n(Cleveland: \"an investor buying in Cleveland.\")\n\n## 2. Permission question\n> \"I want to be respectful of your time. Is this an okay moment to talk for a minute?\"\n\n- **Yes:** go on.\n- **They say the owner has passed:** \"I'm so sorry for your loss. I don't want to intrude. If it's all right, could I ask one or two quick questions about the property, or would another time be better?\"\n- **Bad time / upset:** \"I completely understand. I'm sorry to bother you. May I check back in a few weeks, or would you rather I didn't?\" Then stop.\n\n## 3. Reason for the call (soft, public records)\n> \"The county records still show the property in [Owner's name]'s name. I buy houses, so I wanted to ask whether the family has plans for it. I'm not trying to rush anyone or pressure you.\"\n\n(Never say \"I saw that the owner died\" like a statistic. If they say it, follow their lead. Don't ask how or when the person died.)\n\n## 4. Discovery questions (gentle; one at a time; stop if they need to)\n1. **Who handles it:** \"Who is taking care of [Owner's] affairs or the estate? Is that you, or someone else? Could you point me to that person?\"\n2. **Authority:** \"Has an estate been opened with the probate court? Is there an executor or administrator, or an attorney helping?\" (Don't say who may sign. \"That's a question for the attorney or the court. I'm not a lawyer.\")\n3. **Heirs / decision makers:** \"Is it one person making decisions, or several family members?\" \"Do you all agree about what to do with the house?\"\n4. **Condition / occupancy:** \"How's the property right now? Is anyone living there, or is it empty? Does it need work?\"\n5. **Timeline:** \"Is there a time you'd like this settled, or are you still sorting things out?\"\n6. **Mortgage / liens / taxes:** \"Do you know if there's a mortgage on it or any unpaid taxes or bills attached?\"\n7. **Motivation:** \"What would be the best outcome for the family?\" (Then be quiet.)\n8. **Price expectation (only if they bring it up or the conversation is going well):** \"Have you had it looked at or priced yet?\"\n\n## 5. Setting up the offer (no number on this call unless they push)\n> \"Whenever the family is ready, here's how it works. I buy as-is for cash. Nobody has to clean out or repair anything, and there are no showings. I'd look at the title and the numbers and send a **written offer**. There's no obligation, and nothing happens until the right people have signed, with your attorney's guidance. Would you like me to send it to you, or to the attorney or executor?\"\n\n**If they push for a number** (once, as a range):\n> \"I can't be exact yet. Homes like this usually land between $[LOW] and $[HIGH], depending on title, liens, taxes and condition. That's not an offer.\"\n\n**Authority:** Before any contract, you'll need proof that the signer has authority (e.g. letters of authority from the probate court, or an attorney's confirmation; title company decides what's required). Don't tell them who can sign. **Ohio:** the separate ORC 5301.95 disclosure goes to the record owner or the authorized fiduciary before any binding contract. **NM:** no finder's fees.\n\n## 6. Top 6 objections\n\n**1. \"This isn't a good time. We're still grieving.\"**\n> \"I completely understand, and I'm sorry. There's no rush on my side. May I leave you my number in case it helps later, or would you prefer I not contact you again?\"\n\n**2. \"How did you know / how did you get my number?\"**\n> \"The county's public property records show who owns the home, and I found the contact information through a public lookup. I'm sorry if this felt intrusive. I'll take you off my list right now, if you'd like.\"\n\n**3. \"Probate isn't done / we can't sell yet.\"**\n> \"That makes sense, and I wouldn't want to push you ahead of the process. A lawyer or the court would be the one to say who can sign. If it's helpful later, I'm happy to send a written offer once that's sorted out. Would it be okay to check back in [a few months]?\"\n\n**4. \"We have to ask the rest of the family.\"**\n> \"Of course. I can send something in writing so everyone can read the same thing. Is there one person I should send it to?\"\n\n**5. \"You'll lowball us / we'll list it with an agent.\"**\n> \"That's your choice, and an agent may be right for some families. I'm a buyer, not an agent, and I don't list properties. If an as-is cash sale with no repairs or showings is helpful, I can send a written offer for you to compare. No pressure.\"\n\n**6. \"Are you a wholesaler?\"**\n> \"I'm an investor, and I'm the buyer. I buy as 'Christian Moseley and/or assigns,' so I may assign the contract to another buyer before closing. I'll put that in writing before anyone signs anything. I'm not an agent and I don't represent the family. If the home is in Ohio, there's also a separate disclosure form I'll send for the owner to sign before any contract.\"\n\n## 7. Spoken disclosure (say it before you end any call that is going toward an offer)\n> \"One thing I tell every seller up front: I buy as 'Christian Moseley and/or assigns.' That means I may assign the contract to another buyer before closing. I'll put that in writing in the paperwork I send, before you sign anything. I'm a buyer. I'm not an agent, and I'm not working for you.\"\n> **If the property is in Ohio:** \"Ohio law also requires me to give you a separate disclosure form, and both of us sign and date it before any contract. I'll send it with the paperwork and it explains how I work. Nothing is binding until that is signed.\" (ORC 5301.95, in effect March 2, 2026, 1-4 unit residential: separate document, bold 12-point type, signed and dated by the owner and by you before any binding contract.)\n> **If the property is in New Mexico:** \"I don't charge you any fee or commission, and I'm not finding a buyer for you for a fee.\" (No finder's fees in NM.)\n\n## 8. Voicemail (leave it live, you speaking; no prerecorded or ringless drops; 20 to 25 seconds; no price, no pressure)\n> \"Hi, this is Christian Moseley, a local investor. I'm trying to reach whoever looks after the property at [street name]. There's no rush at all. If it would help to talk about the property, you can reach me at [phone number]. That's Christian Moseley, [phone number]. Thank you, and take care.\"\n\n(Do not mention a death or an estate on a voicemail.)\n\n## 9. Text-free follow-up note (mail or email only; do not text)\nSend only if they did not ask to stop, ideally same day or next. It also puts the assignment disclosure in writing.\n**Subject / top line:** About the property at [street name]\n\n> Dear [Name],\n>\n> This is Christian Moseley, a local investor. [Thank you for speaking with me on [date]. / I tried to reach you on [date].] I'm sorry for your loss, and I don't want to add stress.\n>\n> Whenever the family is ready, I can buy the property as-is, for cash, with no repairs, cleanup or showings. I can send a written offer to you, or to the attorney or executor, if you'd like.\n>\n> In writing: I buy as **\"Christian Moseley and/or assigns,\"** so I may assign the contract to another buyer before closing. I'm a buyer. I'm not an agent or an attorney and I don't represent the family. Anyone signing should have the legal authority to sign, and you're welcome to have an attorney review anything I send. In Ohio, I will also send the separate disclosure required by ORC 5301.95 before any contract.\n>\n> If you'd rather not hear from me, tell me and I'll take you off my list.\n>\n> Christian Moseley\n[phone] | [email]\n[mailing address]\n\n## Do-not-call handling (use the second they ask, in any words)\n> \"Absolutely. I'm taking you off my list right now and you won't hear from me again. Sorry for the interruption. Take care.\"\n\nThen: hang up politely. Mark the number **DNC** in your own list and in the hub right away (same day, before the next dial). Don't ask why, don't make one last pitch, don't call again, and don't send a follow-up note. This also applies to \"stop calling,\" \"take me off,\" \"I'm on the do-not-call list,\" and \"don't contact me.\"\n\n## After the call\n- Log: date and time, outcome, motivation, timeline, price they mentioned, who decides, and any DNC. Put it in the hub notes **without the owner's name or address.**\n- Promised something? Do it the same day (written offer, paperwork, call-back date).\n- If you did not reach them, wait a couple of days before the next try. Cap it at a few tries, spaced out, then stop.\n" },
  foreclosure_tax: { label: "Foreclosure or tax delinquent", title: "Script 7: Foreclosure or tax-delinquent owner (sensitive; no promises of saving the house)", file: "07_foreclosure_tax_delinquent.md", text: "*Caller: Christian Moseley (live, himself). Plain, direct, friendly. Items in [brackets] come from the lead row. Lines in quotes are said out loud.*\n\n## 0. Before you dial (every call)\n- **Live human only.** You dial and talk yourself. No autodialer, no prerecorded or \"ringless\" voicemail, no AI voice.\n- **Scrub the number against the National Do Not Call registry first** (donotcall.gov), and against your own do-not-call list. If it hits either one, do not call.\n- **Time check, 8 AM to 9 PM in the owner's local time.** Use the **owner's local time.** **Eastern (Cleveland): 6 AM to 7 PM Mountain for you. Mountain (NM): 8 AM to 9 PM.** Don't call early or late for this one; stay inside 9 AM to 8 PM. Look at a clock right before you dial. If you are unsure which zone the person is in (out-of-state owner, cell number), stay inside 9 AM to 8 PM in every zone it could be.\n- **Recording:** do not record unless the other person agrees. If you do record, say so at the start (see the line below). Ohio and New Mexico are one-party-consent states, but saying so is safest, and the other person may be in a different state.\n  - Say: \"I'd like to record this call just for my own notes. Is that okay?\" If no, don't record, and take notes by hand.\n- Have the lead row open (address/APN, owner, flags). Write the time you called and the outcome as you go.\n- Know what the records say (tax balance, any court filing or sale date) but **do not recite it.** The owner may be stressed or embarrassed. Neighbors or family may hear.\n- You are **not** a lender, attorney or housing counselor. You cannot stop a foreclosure or tax sale and you never imply you can. Never use the words \"save your house,\" \"stop the foreclosure,\" or \"guarantee.\"\n- Never ask for money up front. Never ask anyone to sign anything on this call.\n\n**Rules for the whole call**\n- You are the **buyer**, always as **\"Christian Moseley and/or assigns.\"** Say it out loud and put it in writing (see the spoken disclosure below).\n- Never say you are an agent or broker. Never offer to list the property or \"find a buyer\" for a fee. You buy directly.\n- Nothing misleading: no made-up deadlines, no \"I have a buyer waiting,\" no \"my partners,\" no hiding that you may assign.\n- If they ask where you're based or whether you're local, answer truthfully and plainly.\n- No pressure. A calm \"no\" is a good outcome. Do not argue.\n\n## 1. Opener\n> \"Hi, is this [First name]? This is Christian Moseley. I'm a local investor.\" (Cleveland: \"an investor buying in Cleveland.\")\n\n## 2. Permission question\n> \"I'm sorry to call out of the blue. Do you have a minute?\"\n\n- **Bad time:** \"No problem. When's better?\"\n- **Upset / suspicious:** \"I understand. I'm not here to add stress, and I won't take much of your time.\"\n- **Wrong person:** \"Sorry about that. Do you know the best way to reach the owner?\"\n\n## 3. Reason for the call (soft, public records)\n> \"I look at public property records for homes in [Cleveland / the area], and yours at [street name] came up. I buy houses as-is, for cash, and I wanted to ask if selling is something you'd ever thought about. I'm not calling to make anything harder.\"\n\n(Do not say \"foreclosure,\" \"delinquent,\" \"behind,\" \"sheriff's sale\" or a dollar amount unless **they** bring it up. If they ask what you saw: \"Just what's public: there's a past-due tax balance or a court filing. I only know what's on paper, and you know your situation far better than I do.\")\n\n## 4. Discovery questions (ask gently; they don't have to answer)\n1. **Situation / motivation:** \"How are things with the house right now? Is selling something you'd consider, or would you rather keep it if you could?\"\n2. **Timeline:** \"Is there a date you're working against, like a court date, a sale date, or a tax deadline? Do you know what it is?\"\n3. **Mortgage / liens:** \"Is there a mortgage? Roughly what's owed, and has the lender or an attorney been in touch?\" \"Any other liens or back taxes?\"\n4. **Help already in place:** \"Have you talked to your lender, an attorney, or a housing counselor? Is there a payment plan or something in the works?\" (If not, give the counselor line below.)\n5. **Condition / occupancy:** \"What shape is the house in? Does anyone live there?\"\n6. **Price expectation:** \"Do you have an idea of what it's worth, or what you'd need to walk away with?\"\n7. **Decision makers:** \"Is it just you on the deed and the loan? Anyone else I'd need to talk to?\"\n\n**Give this to anyone who sounds stressed or unsure (offer, don't push):**\n> \"Before you decide anything, I'd suggest talking to a HUD-approved housing counselor. It's free. The number is 800-569-4287, and the Homeowner's HOPE Hotline is 888-995-4673. They can look at options I can't, like a payment plan or loan help.\" (Check the numbers on hud.gov before you read them. The county treasurer's office may also have a tax payment plan.)\n\n## 5. Setting up the offer (no number on this call unless they push)\n> \"If you'd like me to take a look, here's what I'd do. I'd check the title, the payoff and the numbers, then send you a **written offer**, as-is, for cash. There's no obligation and no fee to you. A sale has to close before any sale date, and I can't promise that works or that it solves everything. A title company would handle the payoffs. Is it okay to send it to [email / mail]? Is there a date I should know about?\"\n\n**If they push for a number** (once, as a range):\n> \"I can't be exact yet. Homes like this usually land between $[LOW] and $[HIGH], depending on what's owed, the title, and the condition. That's not an offer, and I can't tell you whether it will cover what you owe.\"\n\n**Rules for this call**\n- No promise to stop a foreclosure or tax sale, to rescue credit, or that a lender will agree.\n- Never ask for upfront fees, deposits or a deed. Nothing gets signed on this call.\n- Don't push a decision. Suggest they talk to a lawyer or counselor first.\n- **Ohio:** the separate ORC 5301.95 disclosure (bold 12-point, signed and dated by the owner and by you) must be signed **before** any binding contract. **NM:** no finder's fees.\n\n## 6. Top 6 objections\n\n**1. \"Can you stop the foreclosure / save my house?\"**\n> \"I'm sorry, but no. I can't stop it, and I can't promise to save it. I'm not a lender or an attorney. I'm an investor who may buy the house if selling is the right thing for you. If you want to keep it, a housing counselor or an attorney can help far more than I can. If you decide to sell, I can look at an offer. There's no obligation.\"\n\n**2. \"I don't want to sell / I want to keep my house.\"**\n> \"Totally understand. If keeping it is possible, that's often best. I'd call a housing counselor or your lender. If things change and you want a cash offer, you're welcome to call me. Is it okay if I check back, or would you rather I didn't?\"\n\n**3. \"Are you one of those vultures / is this a scam?\"**\n> \"I understand the worry. There are people who take advantage. I'm not asking you for any money, and nobody has to sign anything today. I'm an investor, I'd put anything in writing, and you can have a lawyer look at it first. I buy as 'Christian Moseley and/or assigns,' and I'll tell you everything about that in writing.\"\n\n**4. \"I owe more than it's worth / what if the sale doesn't cover it?\"**\n> \"That's an important question, and I can't promise it works. The lender has to agree to a sale for less than what's owed, and I can't promise they will. A title company would check the payoff and the liens before anything happens. A housing counselor or an attorney can walk you through that.\"\n\n**5. \"How did you find out / how did you get my number?\"**\n> \"County records are public, and I found the number through a public lookup. I'm sorry if that felt intrusive. I'll take you off my list right now, if you'd like.\"\n\n**6. \"I need to think / talk to my lawyer or the bank.\"**\n> \"Please do. That's the right thing. If you'd like, I'll send what I have in writing so you and your lawyer can read the same thing. And if there's a deadline, please don't wait on me. Can I check back on [day]?\"\n\n## 7. Spoken disclosure (say it before you end any call that is going toward an offer)\n> \"One thing I tell every seller up front: I buy as 'Christian Moseley and/or assigns.' That means I may assign the contract to another buyer before closing. I'll put that in writing in the paperwork I send, before you sign anything. I'm a buyer. I'm not an agent, and I'm not working for you.\"\n> **If the property is in Ohio:** \"Ohio law also requires me to give you a separate disclosure form, and both of us sign and date it before any contract. I'll send it with the paperwork and it explains how I work. Nothing is binding until that is signed.\" (ORC 5301.95, in effect March 2, 2026, 1-4 unit residential: separate document, bold 12-point type, signed and dated by the owner and by you before any binding contract.)\n> **If the property is in New Mexico:** \"I don't charge you any fee or commission, and I'm not finding a buyer for you for a fee.\" (No finder's fees in NM.)\n\n## 8. Voicemail (leave it live, you speaking; no prerecorded or ringless drops; 20 to 25 seconds; no price, no pressure)\n> \"Hi, this is Christian Moseley, a local investor. I'm calling for [First name] about the property on [street name]. There's nothing urgent. If you ever thought about selling, I buy as-is for cash. You can reach me at [phone number]. That's Christian Moseley, [phone number]. Thank you.\"\n\n(Do not mention foreclosure, taxes or money on a voicemail.)\n\n## 9. Text-free follow-up note (mail or email only; do not text)\nSend only if they did not ask to stop, ideally same day or next. It also puts the assignment disclosure in writing.\n**Subject / top line:** About the house at [street name]\n\n> Hi [First name],\n>\n> This is Christian Moseley, a local investor. [We spoke on [date]. / I tried to reach you on [date].] Thank you for your time.\n>\n> If you'd like me to take a look, I can send a written, as-is cash offer after I check the title and the numbers. There's no obligation and no fee to you. I can't stop a foreclosure or tax sale or promise any outcome, and I'm not a lender, attorney or housing counselor. A free HUD-approved housing counselor (800-569-4287) can help with options I can't.\n>\n> In writing: I buy as **\"Christian Moseley and/or assigns,\"** so I may assign the contract to another buyer before closing. I'm a buyer. I'm not an agent and I don't represent you. In Ohio, I'll also send the separate disclosure required by ORC 5301.95 before any contract. You're welcome to have an attorney review anything I send.\n>\n> If you'd rather not hear from me, just say so and I'll take you off my list.\n>\n> Christian Moseley\n[phone] | [email]\n[mailing address]\n\n## Do-not-call handling (use the second they ask, in any words)\n> \"Absolutely. I'm taking you off my list right now and you won't hear from me again. Sorry for the interruption. Take care.\"\n\nThen: hang up politely. Mark the number **DNC** in your own list and in the hub right away (same day, before the next dial). Don't ask why, don't make one last pitch, don't call again, and don't send a follow-up note. This also applies to \"stop calling,\" \"take me off,\" \"I'm on the do-not-call list,\" and \"don't contact me.\"\n\n## After the call\n- Log: date and time, outcome, motivation, timeline, price they mentioned, who decides, and any DNC. Put it in the hub notes **without the owner's name or address.**\n- Promised something? Do it the same day (written offer, paperwork, call-back date).\n- If you did not reach them, wait a couple of days before the next try. Cap it at a few tries, spaced out, then stop.\n" },
});
